WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.379 --> 00:00:05.540
Before we begin working with an animation within Unreal, we need to understand some

2
00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:12.580
UE skeleton principles. So every skeleton needs to have one root bone. In gaming engines,

3
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:17.140
this is used to communicate to the engine where your character is within world space.

4
00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:23.660
Off from that root bone, you're going to expand from your hip bone out to arms,

5
00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:28.460
to legs, to head, whatever. But it's very important that this follows the same convention

6
00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:33.740
in every skeleton you build. Now floating bones, they are seen as new skeletons. So if you have a

7
00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:40.140
pistol with an armature and you have your skeleton for your character, they cannot be separated.

8
00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:45.020
Otherwise, it will be seen as a separate element. This goes along with face bones. So if you're

9
00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:49.579
having your face with multiple bones that controls the mesh, you want to make sure

10
00:00:49.579 --> 00:00:55.100
that's attached to the head bone so that Unreal sees it all as the same when bringing it in.

11
00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:59.659
So as this says, you want to start at the hip bone and work your way out from the root.

12
00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:04.299
Thighs and spine from hip, spine into clavicles and neck, and so forth. And again,

13
00:01:04.299 --> 00:01:09.180
face bones attached to the head bone. This is very, very important. Otherwise,

14
00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:12.939
Unreal will see them as a separate skeleton and you'll have a mess to clean up.
